[02:00] ITEM 2 — Pitch Post-Mortem & Feedback Review
Edwin:
"Okay so Item 2 — let's talk about the pitch. Overall I think it went pretty well. But there was one piece of feedback that we need to actually address properly. The marker said our constraints are not really constraints — more like concerns or things we solve ourselves. Sharmanne, you caught most of that feedback — what exactly did they say?"
Sharmanne:
"Yeah so basically they felt that 16GB RAM is something we chose ourselves by going with a local LLM — so it is our own requirement, not really an external constraint. And the data structure inconsistency one — they said that is more of a design challenge we handle in code. Their point was that a real constraint should be something external that limits us regardless of what we decide."
YY:
"Yeah that makes sense actually. Like we can't really call something a constraint if we created the situation ourselves."
Edwin:
"Exactly. So I've been thinking about this and I want to propose two replacements that I think are much more defensible. YY, Wen Jung — tell me what you think.
First one — no access to real student data because of PDPA. Under Malaysia's Personal Data Protection Act, we literally cannot use real Monash student records for development and testing without formal data agreements. So we have to use mock data the whole way through. That directly limits how accurately we can test real-world scenarios. That's external, regulatory, completely out of our hands.
Second one — Monash Handbook scraper dependency. Our system pulls LO data from the Handbook but Monash controls that site. If they change the structure or block scrapers, our data ingestion breaks and we can't do anything about it. That's also why we made it a nice-to-have rather than a core feature — the dependency is too fragile.
Does that feel stronger to everyone?"
YY:
"Yeah definitely. The PDPA one especially — that's a real legal boundary, we genuinely can't work around it."
Wen Jung:
"The scraper one is good too because it actually explains why we scoped it the way we did. It's not just a constraint, it justifies a decision we already made."
Sharmanne:
"And the PDPA one ties nicely into our data privacy argument from the WSM as well. It strengthens the overall story."
Edwin:
"Good, glad everyone agrees. So we're formally replacing the old two with these. Wen Jung can you log that — PDPA data access and Monash Handbook scraper dependency are our two revised constraints, replacing the 16GB RAM and data inconsistency ones. Timestamp it please.
One more thing — does this change anything about our scope or SMART goals? I don't think it does but just want to check."
YY: "No, scope stays the same." Sharmanne: "Agreed, goals are still valid." Wen Jung: "Same from my side."
Edwin:
"Perfect. Scope and goals unchanged. Let's move to Item 3."

